<Article
  title="The Owner’s Position — What You Cannot See From It"
  heading="The owner’s position"
  kicker="Cornerstone"
  description="Why the owner of a significant residential project is the least-informed party at the table, and what an independent owner’s representative actually changes."
>
  <p>
    Commission a significant residence and you become, briefly, the chief executive of a small and expensive
    company. It has a payroll, a supply chain, a legal department, a design office, and a burn rate. You did
    not choose the role and you will hold it for two or three years. Almost everything you know about how the
    company is doing will come to you from people you are paying — which is to say, from people with a
    position in every number they report.
  </p>

  <h2>Everyone at the table is paid by someone</h2>
  <p>
    None of this requires bad actors. It is structure, not character. The general contractor’s project
    manager is competent, personable, and employed by the general contractor: when scope is ambiguous, their
    job is to resolve it in the builder’s favour. The architect cares about the design surviving contact with
    the budget, and will defend the drawings before they defend your contingency. The lender’s inspector
    protects the loan collateral, not your interests as an owner. Your lawyer sees the contract once, before
    signing, and then goes back to their practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each of these people is doing their job. The difficulty is that nobody at the table has, as their job,
    <em>your</em> whole position — cost, schedule, quality, and risk, held together, on your side only. In
    any other domain where this much of your capital moves — a company acquisition, a serious dispute — you
    would not consider walking in without representation of your own. Construction is the last large
    transaction people routinely enter alone.
  </p>

  <h2>What the owner cannot see</h2>
  <p>
    Four things, mostly, and each is invisible from the owner’s chair precisely because the parties who can
    see it have no reason to show you.
  </p>
  <h3>1. Whether the price was ever real</h3>
  <p>
    A construction budget is a stack of assumptions: allowances that may be placeholders, exclusions in the
    fine print, contingencies that belong to the builder rather than to you. Two bids that look alike can
    carry entirely different risk. The moment the contract is signed, the assumptions harden into your
    problem. Reviewing them afterwards is archaeology; reviewing them before is leverage.
  </p>
  <h3>2. What the contract actually allocates</h3>
  <p>
    Construction agreements are drafted by the industry, for the industry. Payment terms, change-order
    mechanics, delay provisions, termination rights — the defaults favour the drafter. An owner reads the
    price and the schedule; the rest of the document decides what happens when either one moves. It is the
    rest of the document that matters.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Whether the work behind a draw exists</h3>
  <p>
    A payment application is a claim, not a fact. It asserts percentages complete, materials stored,
    milestones reached. On most private projects nobody independent ever checks the claim against the site
    before the money moves — the person certifying progress is paid by the party requesting payment. Draw
    verification is the plainest, most mechanical service we provide, and it is the one that changes
    behaviour on a project fastest, because everyone performs differently when they know someone is checking.
  </p>
  <h3>4. What the change orders are doing in aggregate</h3>
  <p>
    No single change order sinks a project. The accumulation does — priced one at a time, approved under
    schedule pressure, each reasonable in isolation. Without someone keeping a running ledger of scope
    against budget, the owner discovers the total at the end, when there is nothing left to decide.
  </p>

  <h2>The timing problem</h2>
  <p>
    Owner attention and owner leverage run in opposite directions. Leverage is highest before signatures —
    when terms can still be negotiated, the team can still be shaped, and walking away costs nothing but
    time. Attention peaks mid-construction, when something visible has gone wrong — and by then the contract
    is fixed, the buyout is done, and the early draws are paid. The expensive mistakes were made quietly, in
    paperwork, months earlier.
  </p>

  <RiskLadder />

  <h2>What representation changes</h2>
  <p>
    An owner’s representative does not perform construction work, does not hire the trades, and holds no
    contract with the builder — you contract directly with your contractor, and we advise, review, verify,
    and report on your side of that line. Within that line, the work is concrete: due diligence on the
    budget and the agreement before you sign; independent verification of every draw; a running account of
    changes against contingency; and a standing report, in plain language, of where the project actually is.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result is not that nothing goes wrong. Things go wrong on every project. The result is that you find
    out early, in writing, from someone whose only client is you — while the options are still open and still
    cheap.
  </p>

